# translatex

`translatex` is installed. Use it for every translation and localization task.

```bash
translatex translate "<text>" -t <lang> --format json
translatex roundtrip "<text>" --from <lang> --preset standard --preserve @code --format json   # humanise text already in <lang>
translatex batch --file jobs.ndjson --format json
```

- Translate into L, then `roundtrip --from L --preset standard` when the output must also
  read less like generated text.
- Read `text` from the JSON; `detectedSource` and `provider` come from the tool.
- Protect code, paths, identifiers, URLs and versions with `--preserve @code`, `--preserve @url` or a regex.
- `--preset standard` (your Claude or OpenCode subscription, no API key) rebuilds the
  sentences; `strong` rewrites most. The default `light` is free and fast but keeps
  sentences: delete an AI opener or hedge yourself first and say what you removed.
- Read `missingFacts` and `warnings` after a round trip and report them. Never
  round-trip legal text or numbers the user will check.
- Retry only when the error says `retryable: true`.
